ood-brain barrier and that's why  glucose could enter the brain and work  like a primary source of energy  glucose undergoes glycolysis kreb cycle  all inside the brain cells and give it  energy and that's what keep us going  right now in case of cancer cell which  are rapidly dividing and their shows  uncontrolled growth they have a urge to  divide and they need huge amount of ATP  for rapid division and how does that it  become and imagine all this Krebs cycle  and all these etc' all this can happen  under aerobic condition but in a tumor  cell which is deeply inside a tumor core  it is in a hypoxic situation not getting  oxygen from the blood vessels because  the blood vessel might not be entering  so deep inside the tumor under that  hypoxic situation glycolysis is the  pathway which gives the tumor cell  energy or the ATP not these Krebs cycle  or the e.t.c so that's why tumor cell  primarily depend upon the glycolytic  pathways for energy and this effect is  known as Warburg effect in different  videos we would be talking about these  Warburg effect so glycolysis is the key  source of energy for rapidly dividing  cells such as cancer cell apart from  cancer cell stem cells also use the same  method to produce rapid energy and that  really helps them to divide now it is a  10 step process and several enzymes are  involved in this process so let's just  not get into too much detail in the  enzymatic reactions so let's just first  look at the important steps two steps  you can see that ATP is invested or its  investment but ATP is utilized and in  later on ATP is generated in further two  steps right so these two steps are known  as payoff phase and first two steps are  investment phase  in the beta phase where 1 3  bisphosphoglycerate get converted to 3  phosphoglycerate and phosphoenolpyruvate  gets converted into pyruvate these are  known as substrate level phosphorylation  because all of these cases ADP is  converted to ATP taking the phosphate  group from the substrates about  substrate level phosphorylation we're  going to talk about separately in a  different video but for now we had a  quick overview of glycolysis so if you  liked this video give it a thumbs up  don't forget to Like share and subscribe  thank you
 

